cyclicCode -- cyclic codes
**************************

  * Usage: 
        cyclicCode(F, g, n)
        cyclicCode(F, m, n)
  * Inputs:
      * F, a *note Galois field: (Macaulay2Doc)GaloisField,, 
      * g, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, 
      * m, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, 
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, 
  * Outputs:
      * an instance of the type *note LinearCode: LinearCode,, $C$

Description
===========

A linear code is called cyclic if $(a_{n},a_1,\ldots,a_{n-1})\in C$ for all
$(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)\in C$. A cyclic code can be defined by a polynomial.

a polynomial is given
=====================

  * Usage: 
        cyclicCode(F,g,n)
  * Inputs:
      * F, a *note Galois field: (Macaulay2Doc)GaloisField,, 
      * g, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, 
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, 

Given a finite field F, an integer n, and a polynomial g in F$[x]\setminus$F
that is a divisor of $x^n-1$, this function returns the cyclic code $C$ with
generating polynomial g and length n.



If the polynomial g is not a divisor of $x^n-1$, the function returns a code
with a circulant matrix as generator matrix.

